+## Evidence
+
+- Original: the repository's `electronics/datasheets/SK6812.pdf`, copied to `datasheets/Original_SK6812.pdf`; LED COLOR, SPC/SK6812, revision 01, seven pages.
+- Replacement: OPSCO `SKC6812RGBW-XX-X-00X`, **revision B/1, 2025-07-31**, downloaded through the exact [C5378724 supplier listing](https://www.lcsc.com/product-detail/RGB-LEDs-Built-in-IC_OPSCO-Optoelectronics-SKC6812RGBW-WS_C5378724.html). The [supplier-linked manufacturer PDF](https://datasheet.lcsc.com/datasheet/pdf/f73fcb028ddf7140b2dbd34804e8ce40.pdf?productCode=C5378724) is retained in `datasheets/OPSCO_SKC6812RGBW_RevB1_2025-07-31.pdf`.
+- Reference date: 2026-10-29. The publicly mirrored B/0 revision was also examined, but **B/1 governs this comparison**. B/1 changes voltage, logic thresholds, temperature limits, and timing details; distributor summary fields may still reflect older revisions. OPSCO's download page did not provide a directly accessible newer specification.
+
+The original PDF describes a **24-bit RGB** LED, while `software/esp32s3/main/main.c` already selects `.is_rgbw = true`. The original hardware's exact RGBW manufacturer part number, current version, and white bin are therefore not established by the supplied design files. The generic name SK6812 alone cannot prove equivalence.
+
+## Pinout and footprint
+
+Original PDF page 3, replacement PDF pages 5-7, embedded schematic symbols, and every PCB LED pad agree:
+
+| Pin | Original | Replacement | Existing PCB connection |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 1 | VSS | GND | GND |
+| 2 | DIN | DIN | Incoming serial data |
+| 3 | VDD | VDD | P, the board's power rail |
+| 4 | DOUT | DOUT | Outgoing serial data |
+
+In the unrotated **top view**, pin 1 is lower right at the chamfer, pin 2 upper right, pin 3 upper left, and pin 4 lower left. Preserve the existing placement rotations; do not use the bottom-view drawing as a placement orientation.
+
+The replacement's nominal package is 5.0 x 4.85 x 1.6 mm, with 3.2 mm vertical terminal pitch. The existing footprint uses 1.5 x 1.0 mm pads centered at X = +/-2.45 mm, Y = +/-1.60 mm. OPSCO page 7 recommends **1.8 x 1.2 mm** pads, 3.3 mm horizontal inner gap and 2.0 mm vertical inner gap (derived centers X = +/-2.55 mm, Y = +/-1.60 mm). The existing pads nominally overlap the replacement terminals, but they are **not the recommended land pattern**. Assembly acceptance of the retained footprint, or a verified footprint revision, is required before fabrication. Pinout compatibility does not itself establish solder-joint suitability.
+
+## Electrical and optical comparison
+
+Source pages: original 3-5; replacement 7-8.
+
+| Parameter | Original supplied SK6812 PDF | OPSCO revision B/1 | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Supply | Nominal 5 V; electrical table references 4.5-5.5 V | 3.7-5.5 V | Nominal 5 V compatible |
+| RGB channel drive current | Not specified | 8 mA typical per channel | Equal current cannot be verified |
+| White channel | Not documented | 16.5 mA typical; 2800-3200 K | Different documented function |
+| Idle current | 1 mA typical | 0.29 mA typical | Different |
+| PWM frequency | 1.2 kHz typical | 4 kHz typical | Different |
+| Input thresholds at 5 V | 3.4/1.6 V typical switching thresholds | VIH >= 3.25 V; VIL <= 1.75 V | Different specifications |
+| RGB intensity, mcd | R 700-1000; G 1500-2200; B 700-1000 | R 200-385; G 515-932.5; B 160-320 | Same brightness is not established |
+| Operating temperature | -40 to +85 C | -40 to +80 C | Reduced upper limit |
+| Storage temperature | -50 to +150 C | -40 to +80 C | Different |
+| ESD rating | 4 kV listed | 2 kV HBM | Not an equivalent rating |
+
+The original's 49/-50 mA figures describe **DOUT pin drive**, not LED-channel or package current. They cannot be used to claim equal power consumption. The original optical table omits test current, preventing a controlled brightness comparison.
+
+At full-scale R+G+B+W, an estimate from the replacement's typical channel and idle currents is `3*8 + 16.5 + 0.29 = 40.79 mA` per LED. For the firmware's 361 pixels this is approximately **14.73 A at 5 V (73.6 W)**, before the Raspberry Pi and other electronics. That exceeds the original electronics README's 5 V / 10 A supply. This is a typical-current estimate, not a guaranteed maximum. Existing brightness restrictions and actual operating loads must be established before accepting the original power budget; no supply or brightness settings were changed here.
+
+## Control compatibility
+
+Replacement pages 9-10 specify GRBW, MSB first, 32 bits per pixel. The existing `led_strip.c` already emits that order when `is_rgbw` is true. Frame format therefore matches the current firmware, despite the original RGB-only PDF.
+
+Timing does **not** fully match:
+
+| Timing | Existing firmware | Replacement B/1 requirement |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| T0H | 300 ns | 250-400 ns |
+| T0L | 900 ns | >=800 ns |
+| T1H | **600 ns** | **650-1000 ns** |
+| T1L | 600 ns | >=200 ns |
+| Bit period | 1200 ns | >=1200 ns |
+| Guaranteed reset delay | **50 us** | **>200 us** |
+
+Application/network delays may lengthen the observed reset, but do not guarantee the required minimum in the driver. A minimal future timing adaptation could use T1H = 750 ns, T1L = 500 ns and reset = 300 us, retaining the current zero-bit timings. This is a recommendation only: firmware edits and flashing are outside this component-only deliverable. Verify real pulse widths, first/last-pixel behavior, and rail voltage with the selected production lot.
+
+## Validation and remaining limits
+
+`validation.json` records successful structural comparisons: all 145 schematic/PCB reference pairs match; all 580 pad numbers/functions match the datasheet; all pad geometry and nets are preserved; only LED identity/documentation and the RGBW symbol label changed. Original project files and software were checked unchanged by SHA-256.
+
+The KiCad connector was bound to a different workspace, and a native KiCad CLI was unavailable when final validation was attempted. Native DRC/ERC, schematic rendering, assembly-process approval, firmware build, and real-hardware measurements were not performed. Structural checks are not substitutes for those checks. This folder cannot honestly be described as an identical-spec or fabrication-approved replacement.
